# Research checklist

Sources follow `data-boundaries.mdc` (public pages only).

## Row format
Every row has: `item`, `value`, `source_url`, `checked_date` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), `status` (`found` | `not_found`).
- `found`: a value plus the exact URL it came from, plus the date checked.
- `not_found`: the words "not found" plus the URL(s) that were checked.
- An item may have sub-rows (e.g. R10a, R10b), each in the same format.

## Items
| # | Item | Must have a value? |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | Legal entity name and HQ | Yes |
| R2 | What the product does (one line) | No |
| R3 | Data it would touch | Yes |
| R4 | Security certifications / trust page | Yes |
| R5 | Audit report access (e.g. SOC 2 Type II) | No |
| R6 | Sub-processor list | No |
| R7 | Data residency options | No |
| R8 | SSO / MFA | No |
| R9 | DPA and breach-notice terms | No |
| R10 | Public pricing + estimated pilot cost | Yes (a "Talk to Sales" tier is fine if another tier is priced) |
| R11 | AI / model use of customer data | No (optional) |

## Enough research means
- R1-R10 each have a row. R11 is optional.
- Every row has a `source_url`.
- R1, R3, R4 and R10 are `found`. If not, the pack goes to a person before routing.

## Never invent a link
- Every `source_url` must appear in the run's fetch log (URL, HTTP status, time) with HTTP 200.
- If a page can't be fetched, the item is `not_found`. Do not guess a URL.
- Starter pages to look for: pricing, trust or security, sub-processor list, DPA, main services agreement, data residency. The legal name and sub-processors are often only in legal or help-center pages.

## Pilot cost (R10)
- Estimate = seats × monthly price × months. Label it `[ASSUMPTION]`.
- Use monthly prices for a pilot. Annual prices mean a 12-month commitment.
- Read the pack as a whole: if a finding limits which tier is acceptable (e.g. data residency only on a higher tier), estimate with that tier.

## Cross-check
Compare intake `touches_customer_data` with R3. If they disagree, flag it (see `approval-policy.mdc`).
